# Kiosk hosted live demos — Caddy reverse proxy
#
# One vhost per demo subdomain -> a loopback Puma (systemd unit, see
# kiosk-demo@.service). Caddy handles automatic TLS (Let's Encrypt HTTP-01)
# per subdomain with zero cert plumbing.
#
# ── DRIFT NOTE (K-463) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file is the canonical template for a FRESH, single-purpose box (it uses
# a `(kioskproxy)` snippet that adds `encode zstd gzip`). The current live VPS,
# however, is a SHARED host: its /etc/caddy/Caddyfile also serves other sites,
# was hand-maintained BEFORE this snippet existed, and uses the plainer inline
# form `reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:PORT` (no snippet). So do NOT overwrite the live
# Caddyfile with this file — you would drop the other sites' vhosts. When adding
# a vhost to the EXISTING live box, hand-add an inline block matching its style
# (that is how kyc.demo.kiosk.tech was added). Bringing the live box up to this
# snippet template (to gain `encode`) is a maintenance-window task, not a copy.
#
# Port map (must match env/<app>.env PORT and kiosk-demo@.service):
#   getgrocery -> 3001   atablefor -> 3002   hoteling  -> 3003
#   skooti     -> 3004   stylish   -> 3005   philslist -> 3006
#   tudu       -> 3007   kyc(prove)-> 3008
#
# ── TLS alternatives ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The per-subdomain blocks below get an individual HTTP-01 cert each — nothing
# extra to configure. If you prefer ONE cert for everything, use a wildcard
# *.demo.kiosk.tech cert via DNS-01 instead. That needs a DNS-provider plugin
# build of Caddy and an API token, e.g.:
#
#   *.demo.kiosk.tech {
#     tls {
#       dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN}   # or your provider's module
#     }
#     @getgrocery host getgrocery.demo.kiosk.tech
#     handle @getgrocery { reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001 }
#     ... one matcher per app ...
#   }
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Global options: contact address for Let's Encrypt expiry notices.
# Set a real ops mailbox before going live.
{
	email ops@kiosk.tech
}

# Reusable snippet: shared proxy + the per-IP edge rate-limit.
#
# ── THE EDGE RATE-LIMIT IS REQUIRED, NOT GARNISH (K-540) ─────────────────────
# Do NOT read the commented lines below as "optional, the PoW covers it". PoW
# does NOT cover it, and this note previously said it did.
#
# `POST /kiosk/auth/register` runs the PoW gate UNAUTHENTICATED, ahead of any
# key verification: anyone can take a free 402 challenge and resubmit it with a
# valid HMAC sig but garbage indices, and every such submission costs THIS box
# an Equihash verify (measured ~19 ms at n=168 k=7). PoW prices the attacker's
# SOLVE; it does not price our VERIFY — on the register path the toll is the
# sink, not the shield. At the shipped WEB_CONCURRENCY=1 roughly 54 req/s
# saturate a worker, and a plain flood of any other endpoint does much the same.
# The app-side mitigations (one issued challenge drives at most one verify;
# structural pre-checks before the hash loop) raise the cost per request; only
# something in FRONT of the app bounds the request RATE. Deploy without one and
# a single laptop can take the box down.
#
# ── Why the directive ships commented out ────────────────────────────────────
# `rate_limit` is NOT a stock Caddy directive — it comes from the third-party
# module github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit. A stock binary REFUSES to load a
# config naming it ("unrecognized directive: rate_limit" — verified on Caddy
# v2.11.2), so uncommenting it without the module means Caddy does not start and
# NO site serves at all. Shipping it live would trade a DoS gap for a guaranteed
# outage, so enabling it is a required RUNBOOK step (deploy/README.md step 4)
# rather than a default in this file.
#
# TO DEPLOY — do both, before `systemctl reload caddy`:
#
#   1. Get a Caddy that HAS the module, e.g.
#        sudo caddy add-package github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit   # Caddy >= 2.7
#        sudo systemctl restart caddy
#      or build one:  xcaddy build --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit
#      Confirm:       caddy list-modules | grep rate_limit
#   2. Uncomment `import ratelimit` in (kioskproxy) AND the whole (ratelimit)
#      snippet below, then `caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile`.
#
# ALTERNATIVE (equally acceptable): a per-IP rate rule on `/kiosk/*` at a CDN /
# WAF in front of this box. What is NOT acceptable is deploying with neither.
(kioskproxy) {
	encode zstd gzip
	# import ratelimit          # REQUIRED — uncomment once the module is installed (see above)
	# Caddy forwards X-Forwarded-For/Proto/Host to the upstream by default.
	reverse_proxy {args[0]}
}

# (snippet) per-IP 60 req/min backstop — requires github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit.
# Uncomment together with the `import ratelimit` line above. 60/min per IP is
# far above any honest agent's pace (register once, then solve a ~1-10 s PoW per
# tolled call) and far below what it takes to saturate a worker.
# (ratelimit) {
# 	rate_limit {
# 		zone kiosk_per_ip {
# 			key    {remote_host}
# 			events 60
# 			window 1m
# 		}
# 	}
# }

getgrocery.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3001
}

atablefor.demo.kiosk.tech {
	# Flagship "book a table for two".
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3002
}

hoteling.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3003
}

skooti.demo.kiosk.tech {
	# "beware: intensive PoW" demo — high register difficulty (see env).
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3004
}

stylish.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3005
}

philslist.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3006
}

tudu.demo.kiosk.tech {
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3007
}

kyc.demo.kiosk.tech {
	# prove.my anonymizing KYC broker (kiosk-demo-prove). An ISSUER, not a Kiosk
	# operator — a human explainer + the /verify page + the operator intake/
	# callback JSON. No PoW, no /.well-known/kiosk.json, no agent surface.
	import kioskproxy 127.0.0.1:3008
}
